  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Above normal temperatures and dry conditions \nwill prevail through Friday. \n\nRidging aloft will gradually work to the east across the region\ntoday through Thursday. The ridge will then get pushed offshore\non Friday ahead of a sharpening trough moving across the Upper \nMidwest and the Great Lakes region. At the surface, dry high \npressure will prevail through Thursday before moving out over \nthe Atlantic Friday as a strong cold front approaches from the \nnorthwest. Warm and dry is a great way to summarize the forecast\nthrough Friday, with daily highs rising into the mid to upper \n80s each day. We could approach daily records at times, \nespecially today and Thursday. See Climate section below for \ndetails. Also of note, there will be the potential for at least \npatchy fog each morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Expect gusty winds and temperatures well below \nnormal this weekend behind a strong cold front.\n\nA strong cold will pass through the region late Friday night \ninto Saturday morning, yielding afternoon temperatures into the \nupper 60s and low 70s through the upcoming weekend. Model \nguidance continues to indicate that it should be a dry cold \nfront with only sprinkles across portions of the Charleston Tri-\nCounty late Friday night into early Saturday morning. This \ncombined with dewpoints in the upper 40s to low 50s will lead to\nfairly nice fall-like weekend. Also, inland surface ridging \nwill yield breezy north-northeasterly flow at 15-20 mph with \ngusts up to 25-30 mph on Saturday and Sunday. This surface ridge\nwill slide offshore of the New England coastline on Monday, \nresulting in the breezy winds to slowly subside and shift more \neasterly. Temperatures on Monday will warm back up into the mid \nto upper 70s.",
